The high-level security consultations in Tehran among Pakistan, Saudi Arabia, Türkiye and Iran mark a pivotal moment for endogenous regional security. Proposed during Field Marshal Asim Munir’s recent visit, this gathering of Saudi Defence Minister Prince Khalid bin Salman, Türkiye’s Intelligence Chief İbrahim Kalın, Pakistan’s Army Chief and Iran’s SNSC Secretary Maj Gen Mohsen Rezaei underscores a shared recognition that lasting stability must be shaped by the region’s own powers through dialogue, not external imposition.
Pakistan’s strong brotherly bond and deep reverence for the Kingdom of Saudi Arabia, exemplary strategic partnership with brotherly Türkiye, and longstanding friendship and neighbourly ties with Iran provide a natural foundation for a coexistence framework. Iran’s constructive engagement can help ease challenges to Saudi security and reduce risks to vital energy chokepoints from Bab al-Mandab to the Strait of Hormuz. Prolonged conflict risks pushing oil prices beyond $130 a barrel and deepening the global economic crisis — making the restoration of stable energy trade an urgent shared interest.
Such a regional approach can de-escalate tensions, advance collective prosperity and reinforce Muslim unity. No territory should be used to undermine that unity, and the collective wisdom of regional states is needed to protect the region and counter destabilising designs, including the Zionist agenda of Greater Israel.
It is time for the region to breathe. Ending cycles of confrontation through structured cooperation offers the most credible path to peace, stability and shared progress.
